 the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139545750 17592186576411 true] [17592186262763 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\,35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-0.630\\,85$ and $x≈0.416\\,35.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are ${x}_{1}≈-0.630\\,85$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.416\\,35.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.630\\,85\\cdot 0.416\\,35≈-0.26\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139545750 17592186576411 false] [17592186262763 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\