re, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det (E)=-2.\n$$" 13194139545750 17592186576411 false] [17592186265879 1074 "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{\\frac{7}{4}}} & {\\color{lightgray}{\\frac{3}{5}}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\right)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det (E)=-2.\n$$" 13194139545750 17592186576411 true] [17592186265934 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of questions Michael answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions on the exam\n\n- the probability of choosing a correct answer is $p=\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{1}{4}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random vari