am above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=-1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le {x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f707a71f68f2e030696029d9a919e16ae6afda18a1d60dc3f8113f03143243e7.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)[{\\int }_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-1}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{4}+4x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{4}+4(0))-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{4}+4(-1))\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545750 17592186576411 true] [17592186268905 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-54x-30 \\\\  & =6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6(2x+1)(x-5)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=6(2x+1)(x-5)$ has roots at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5,$ as