 =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{11}{4}\\right)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{11}{4}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={\\left(\\frac{11}{4}\\right)}^{2}≈7.56.\n$$" 13194139545750 17592186576411 true] [17592186275164 1074 "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](images/a8/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $12.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{12}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{24}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6*