$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545750 17592186576411 false] [17592186281497 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is con